    <briefdescription>
<para>Functions for simulating networks. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para><simplesect kind="par"><title>Simulation Behaviour</title><para>Simulation starts at the local simulation time of a network i.e., the first simulation starts at t = 0, and subsequent simulations on the same network start at whatever time the previous simulation finished. Each network essentially functions as its own state machine, and because networks can dynamically change during simulation, only the current simulation state is preserved. Any errors encountered during simulation thus leave the network in the state it was in before the error occured. The evaluation order for a single Runge-Kutta stage is all vertices from ndxlGetNetworkFirstVertex to ndxlGetNetworkLastVertex, followed by all edges from ndxlGetNetworkFirstEdge to ndxlGetNetworkLastEdge.</para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="par"><title>Callback Invocation</title><para>Network callbacks are invoked once at local simulation time t = 0, before integration begins, and subsequently after every successful integration step. The initial callback is only invoked once during the lifetime of a network; repeated calls to simulation functions do not invoke it again. Agent callbacks are invoked after every successful integration step, but are not invoked at the initial simulation time. Like stepping, vertex callbacks are evaluated first, then edge callbacks, in the same order as described above.</para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="par"><title>External Failure</title><para>Any callback, dynamics, or noise function returning any nonzero value indicates failure. NDXL immediately terminates the simulation upon receiving this signal. The network state is not rolled back following a callback failure. Instead, it remains at the state reached when the callback was invoked, with all previously completed integration steps and the network configuration preserved.</para>
</simplesect>
